@prefix tmk:     <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/> .
@prefix tmkc:    <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/concept/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/provenance>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "TM-Knowledge — provenance module"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """ADR-0011's field list as RDF, over PROV-O.

The rule this module exists to make enforceable: an assertion that cannot say
where it came from does not get published. Every approved relationship in the
graph appears twice — once as a direct triple so SPARQL is natural, and once as
a tmk:Assertion carrying the passage, the span, the hash, the reviewer and the
date. A SHACL shape fails if the first exists without the second (ADR-0058).

The field list is deliberately open (ADR-0041): Stage 2 has not run, so what a
machine-generated candidate needs to carry is not yet known from evidence. What
is here is what the approved records actually carry today."""@en-AU ;
    owl:imports <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/document> ;
    owl:versionInfo "draft — not approved" .

tmk:Assertion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf prov:Entity ;
    rdfs:label "Assertion"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "This project's record that something was extracted from a passage, by some method, and reviewed by someone. Not the proposition itself — the act of asserting it, with everything an audit needs."@en-AU .

tmk:ApprovedAssertion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:label "Approved assertion"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "An assertion a named human approved on a date. The only kind that may sit in the approved named graph (CLAUDE.md rule 4)."@en-AU .

tmk:AuthoredAssertion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:label "Authored assertion"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """An assertion a machine wrote and no expert has validated (ADR-0079, ADR-0080).

Deliberately NOT a subclass of tmk:ApprovedAssertion and deliberately not in the approved named graph. It is the same shape as one — same subject, predicate, object, passage, span and hash — and differs in exactly the fact that matters: nobody signed it. A query written against tmk:ApprovedAssertion therefore cannot pick one of these up by accident, which is the whole point of giving it its own class rather than a flag.

It carries tmk:reviewStatus "unreviewed" and never "approved": approval moves a record to eval/gold/ through tmk-transcribe, and a record that has moved is rebuilt as a tmk:ApprovedAssertion on the next build (ADR-0086)."""@en-AU .

tmk:CandidateAssertion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:label "Candidate assertion"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "UNPOPULATED. Machine output awaiting a human. Kept in the candidates named graph and never in the approved one; if the two mix once, no later audit can unmix them (ADR-0007)."@en-AU .

tmk:InferredAssertion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:label "Inferred assertion"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Produced by an approved CONSTRUCT rule, never authored. Must identify its source facts and the rule that produced it: an inference that cannot explain itself does not get published."@en-AU .

# --- what an assertion says -------------------------------------------------

tmk:assertionSubject a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:label "assertion subject"@en-AU .

tmk:assertionPredicate a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:label "assertion predicate"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The relation asserted, as a node. Restricted to tmk:ApprovedRelation by a shape, which is how relations.ttl stays a closed list in practice and not only in prose."@en-AU .

tmk:assertionObject a rdf:Property , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:label "assertion object"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The object of the triple this assertion records, as rdf:object is: a node for an object property, a literal for a datatype one. An inference's assertion and its emitted triple agree exactly (the second review response, R04): RULE-0001 emits `?chunk tmk:mixedAuthority true`, so its assertion's object is `true`, not the chunk."@en-AU .

# --- where it came from -----------------------------------------------------

tmk:sourcePassage a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "source passage"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """'Every approved proposition has a source passage' — the first of the six starting SHACL rules.

Not functional, and it has no domain (S036, the review response F01). A record resting
on a Manual chunk and a section of the Act carries both; declared functional, OWL
would make the two passages the same individual, which is the separation of law and
practice undone. It is stamped on concepts and typing records as well as assertions,
so a domain would reclassify them."""@en-AU .

tmk:supportingText a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "supporting text"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The sentence that states it, verbatim. Checked against the snapshot at the recorded offsets: if the text at the span is not this string, the record is stale or the surface was retyped rather than copied (ADR-0045)."@en-AU .

# No domain on the span and hash properties since S041: an evidence anchor carries
# them as well as an assertion, and a domain would reclassify every anchor as an
# assertion (the second review response, R03).

tmk:spanStart a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "span start"@en-AU .

tmk:spanEnd a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "span end"@en-AU .

tmk:sourceContentHash a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "source content hash"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The hash the passage carried when the assertion was made. Staleness is this not matching the pinned snapshot, and a stale assertion returns to review rather than being carried forward — the whole mechanism behind Stage 10."@en-AU .

# --- each evidence item, as a node (S041, the second review response R03) ----
#
# A record names the passages it rests on with tmk:sourcePassage, a shortcut for
# navigation. What it rests on exactly — which sentence, in which version of the
# passage, in what words — is an evidence anchor, one per item of the record's
# `evidence:` list, minted as `<record IRI>/evidence/<n>` (IDENTIFIERS.md §2).

tmk:EvidenceAnchor a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence anchor"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "One item of the evidence a machine-written record rests on: the passage (tmk:sourcePassage, tmk:upstreamRef), the measured span (tmk:spanStart, tmk:spanEnd), the hash the passage carried when it was read (tmk:sourceContentHash), the quoted words (tmk:quote), and the snapshot the build checked that hash against (tmk:checkedAgainst, tmk:isStale). Not an assertion: it states nothing, it locates."@en-AU .

tmk:evidence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range tmk:EvidenceAnchor ;
    rdfs:label "evidence"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "An evidence item of this record. No domain: concepts, typings, relationships, corrections and definitions all carry one."@en-AU .

tmk:quote a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:EvidenceAnchor ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "quote"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The words at the span, verbatim, as the record copied them. The harness checks them against the snapshot."@en-AU .

tmk:checkedAgainst a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:EvidenceAnchor ;
    rdfs:range tmk:DocumentVersion ;
    rdfs:label "checked against"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The snapshot this build compared the anchor's hash with. Not the version the record's author read: that is whichever version carried the recorded hash, and tmk:isStale says whether it is this one."@en-AU .

# --- how it was made --------------------------------------------------------

tmk:extractionMethod a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "extraction method"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A **set**, not a scalar: a term found by three extractors is one assertion with
three methods, because agreement across methods is the confidence signal the
ensemble exists to produce, and it is only visible if the methods land on one
record (ADR-0019, ADR-0020).

Today every value is `expert_elicitation` — no Stage 2 run has happened
(ADR-0010)."""@en-AU .

tmk:model a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "model"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The model that produced the assertion, with its version. Absent on an expert-elicited record. HANDOFF Q3 is the question of which model may be named here at all."@en-AU .

tmk:confidence a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "confidence"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A confidence in the **extraction**. Legitimate and required (CLAUDE.md rule 8).

A confidence attached to a legal conclusion is PU-0003 and is prohibited: no
probability in this system is calibrated against examination outcomes, and none
could be without a decision corpus."""@en-AU .

tmk:reviewStatus a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "review status"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "`approved` | `candidate` | `rejected` | `stale`. Never absent, and never defaulted to `approved`."@en-AU .

tmk:approvedBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "approved by"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The name of the person who approved it. A name and a date are the approval artefact, and there is no separate register (ADR-0039)."@en-AU .

tmk:approvedDate a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "approved date"@en-AU .

tmk:tier a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "tier"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "1 deterministic · 2 low-risk · 3 legally significant (ADR-0008). An expert's call; the ontology only knows there are three."@en-AU .

tmk:modality a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "modality"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """`must` | `may` | `should`, where the relation is normative.

**Absent means not supplied**, and is reported as a gap. It is never inferred
from the sentence's grammar: whether a 'may' is possibility or permission is a
legal reading. Five approved relationships carry no modality today and the graph
carries none for them."""@en-AU .

tmk:appliesWhen a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdf:langString ;
    rdfs:label "applies when"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """The condition under which the supporting sentence states the relationship, in that sentence's own words: 'an application to record assignment is received after the documentation required under reg 16.2 has been sent to the Commission'. The antecedent only; how strongly is tmk:hedge (S041).

On the reified assertion only. The direct triple is a navigation edge and says nothing of when it holds, so a reader who relies on an edge reads its assertion (ADR-0058), and nothing may be inferred from the direct edges (ADR-0134). Absent means no condition was recorded, not that none exists in the law (the review response, F14 and F19)."""@en-AU .

tmk:hedge a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdf:langString ;
    rdfs:label "hedge"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """How strongly the supporting sentence states the relationship, in its own words: 'usually', 'rarely', 'Generally', 'not likely'. Split from tmk:appliesWhen in S041, where five records held a hedge or an outcome in place of a condition (the second review response, R01). A hedge is not a predicate: a relationship the sentence states 'usually' is the same relationship, said less strongly, and a test record can read the word where it is copied."""@en-AU .

tmk:conditionState a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "condition state"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Whether a relationship's condition is known: `stated` (tmk:appliesWhen holds it), `none_stated` (its sentence holds no conditional marker), `assessed_none` (it holds one, and tmk:noConditionBecause says why it does not condition this relationship) or `not_assessed`. Computed by rule (`ontology.conditions`), never written by hand, so "no condition" and "nobody looked" are never the same (the second review response, R07)."""@en-AU .

tmk:noConditionBecause a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdf:langString ;
    rdfs:label "no condition because"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Why the conditional marker in a relationship's sentence does not condition it: a reader's judgement, on the record it qualifies."@en-AU .

tmk:proceeding a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "proceeding"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The proceeding a relationship's Manual passage is about, read off its Part by a lookup (`ontology.conditions.PROCEEDING_BY_PART`). Absent where the Part covers several proceedings or none in particular, or the source is legislation: absent is not 'every proceeding'."@en-AU .

tmk:markType a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "mark type"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The kind of trade mark a relationship's Manual passage is about where its Part fixes one: collective (Part 33), defensive (Part 34), certification (Part 35). Absent elsewhere."@en-AU .

# --- inference --------------------------------------------------------------

tmk:producedByRule a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:InferredAssertion ;
    rdfs:label "produced by rule"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "'Every inferred result identifies the rule that produced it' — one of the six starting SHACL rules, and the reason a rule file carries an id."@en-AU .

tmk:derivedFrom a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf prov:wasDerivedFrom ;
    rdfs:label "derived from"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The source facts the inference stands on. Plural, and complete: an explanation that names some of its premises is not an explanation. No domain: a reasoning expectation names its expected conclusion's basis with it too, and is not an inference (S036)."@en-AU .

tmk:requiresHumanReview a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:InferredAssertion ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "requires human review"@en-AU .


# --- properties the candidate rules derive ---------------------------------
# Declared here rather than in the module the rule reads from, because they are
# produced by inference and exist nowhere else. A rule deriving an undeclared
# property is a rule writing into a vocabulary nobody agreed.

tmk:restsOn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "rests on"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Derived by RULE-0003 (candidate until the owner approves it, OQ-0030). This record, signed or machine-written, names that passage, provision or unit, in any field or through any element of its own, so it would need re-reading if the source changed. The graph form of the dependency index (`ontology.dependencies`, the second review response R05 and A10); the two are held to the same pairs by a test.

Recorded dependency only. A nil result means no record names the source, never that nothing is affected (Q-28). Keyed by any ref: a query from a section reaches the records resting on its subsections through dcterms:isPartOf."""@en-AU .

tmk:dependencyVia a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "dependency via"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "On a RULE-0003 inference: the property by which the record names the source (tmk:sourcePassage, tmk:legislativeBasis, tmk:definitionSource, tmk:typingBasis, tmk:onlyForGround on an element, ...). Several where it names it several ways."@en-AU .

tmk:dependantStore a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "dependant store"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "On a RULE-0003 inference: `signed` (the record is in the approved graph) or `authored` (machine-written). Never summed (ADR-0080 c3)."@en-AU .

tmk:mixedAuthority a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "carries both law and practice"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Derived by RULE-0001. The passage contains both quoted legislative text and
the Registrar's own practice, so any output derived from it must keep the two
distinguishable.

This is GX-0001's expected inference, and the reason it matters is CQ-0001's
caveat: the Manual quotes s 43 in full inside TMM/Part29/1#1, so an answer can
appear to cite the Act while having read only the Manual. That is a grounding
failure that will not show up as a missing source."""@en-AU .

tmk:impactedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "impacted by"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Derived by RULE-0002. This approved record would have to be re-read if that
passage changed, because the record was read out of it or names it as a
definition source.

Recorded dependency only. A passage that discusses the same subject without
being cited by any record is invisible to it (Q-28), so a nil result means
'nothing recorded depends on this', never 'nothing is affected'."""@en-AU .

# --- what a machine wrote, and on what -------------------------------------
#
# None of the provenance stamps below, nor tmk:confidence, tmk:reviewStatus,
# tmk:approvedBy or tmk:approvedDate above, has a domain. They are written on
# concepts, typing records and evaluation records as well as on assertions, and
# under OWL a domain classifies every subject of its property: declared on
# tmk:Assertion, each made every concept an assertion (S036, the review response
# F01). What a node must carry is checked by shapes/provenance.ttl, which is a
# check and not an inference. tests/unit/test_declarations.py holds the graph to
# its declarations.
#
# ADR-0079 rewrote CLAUDE.md rule 1 from "an agent may not author legal content"
# to "an agent may not launder it". These five properties are what makes the
# second rule mechanical in RDF: an authored assertion that reaches a reader
# carries who wrote it, when, on what evidential footing, why that reading, and
# what to check first. A node missing any of them fails a shape rather than
# being served.

tmk:origin a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "origin"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Which store this node was built from: \"approved\" for eval/gold/, \"authored\" for authored/. Stamped on every node of both, so a triple lifted out of its named graph still says where it came from. The named graph is the governance boundary; this is the belt to its braces (ADR-0080)."@en-AU .

tmk:authoredBy a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "authored by"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The model that wrote this, with its version. Never a person's name — a person's name goes in tmk:approvedBy and only a transcription from a signed workbook puts it there (ADR-0087)."@en-AU .

tmk:authoredDate a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:date ;
    rdfs:label "authored date"@en-AU .

tmk:authoringBasis a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "authoring basis"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "What the judgement rests on: \"corpus_explicit\" — the corpus states it in terms; \"corpus_inferred\" — the corpus supports it but the reading is the machine's; \"general_knowledge\" — the corpus does not say it. The third is permitted and flagged, never hidden: an unevidenced assertion is not thereby wrong, it is thereby unevidenced, and no count may present it as equivalent to an evidenced one (ADR-0079 guard 2)."@en-AU .

tmk:authoringReasoning a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdf:langString ;
    rdfs:label "authoring reasoning"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Why this reading and not the obvious alternative, written for the expert who will disagree with it. The field that makes correction cheap, which is the entire premise of ADR-0079."@en-AU .

tmk:expertShouldCheck a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range rdf:langString ;
    rdfs:label "expert should check"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The specific thing most likely to be wrong here. A pointer, not a disclaimer."@en-AU .

tmk:supersedesRejected a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "supersedes rejected record"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The id of a record a named expert rejected, where this authored assertion covers the same ground. A signed rejection is a human decision and ADR-0079 does not license reversing one silently: naming it, with the difference explained in tmk:authoringReasoning, is the only way an authored record may occupy that ground (ADR-0084 consequence 2)."@en-AU .

# --- corrections to signed records (ADR-0122) -------------------------------
#
# The owner ruled on 2026-10-08 that known defects in signed records are corrected
# now, and that nobody signs anything until the ontology ships to a group of
# examiners (ADR-0120, ADR-0121). eval/gold/ stays frozen as the yardstick, and a
# change inside a signature would be laundering, so a correction is a record of its
# own: machine-written, unreviewed, naming what it corrects and the ruling it acts on.

tmk:Correction a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:label "Correction to a signed record"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A machine-written change to a signed record, made on the owner's instruction (ADR-0122). Lives in the authored graph and carries the authoring envelope. What it withdrew is recorded on it as text (tmk:withdrawnValue) and stated nowhere that serves; what it added is stated on the corrected node in the authored graph, never the approved one."@en-AU .

tmk:corrects a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Correction ;
    rdfs:label "corrects"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The signed concept, relationship assertion or entity mention this correction changes."@en-AU .

tmk:ruling a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Correction ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "ruling"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The owner's ruling the correction acts on — a ruling file and the item."@en-AU .

tmk:withdrawnValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Correction ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "withdrawn value"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A value the signed record carries that no longer serves, as `field: value`. Text, so the withdrawn statement is recorded without being restated."@en-AU .

tmk:addedValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Correction ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "added value"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A value the correction adds, as `field: value` — the audit trail of a triple the correction states on the corrected node in the authored graph."@en-AU .

tmk:replacedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "replaced by"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The authored relationship assertion that carries the corrected triple in place of a signed one. On the signed assertion it is computed, like tmk:isStale; the signed assertion stays as history and its direct triple stops serving."@en-AU .

tmk:isCorrected a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "is corrected"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Computed on a signed assertion a correction replaced. True means: signed as stated, no longer served as stated."@en-AU .

tmk:kindOfAffirmed a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:AuthoredAssertion ;
    rdfs:range rdf:langString ;
    rdfs:label "kind of, affirmed beside a near-miss"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "On a machine-written 'is a kind of' assertion whose two concepts list each other's names as not the same concept: the written reason both statements hold, from authored/kind-of-affirmed.yaml (review D4, ADR-0125). A narrower idea is never the same idea as its broader one, so a not-label can stand beside a hierarchy; without this reason the SHACL gate fails the assertion. Machine-written and unreviewed."@en-AU .
